## Repeating Code with `while`

`if` runs code once (or not at all). `while` runs code over and over — as long as a condition holds.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var count = 0;

  while count < 5 {
    io.println("Hello!");
    count = count + 1;
  }

  return 0;
}
```

This prints "Hello!" five times.

### How while Works

```
1. Check condition (count < 5)
2. If TRUE → run the body
3. Go back to step 1
4. If FALSE → exit the loop
```

### The Loop Variable Must Change

```xiom
// This loop runs exactly 10 times
var i = 0;
while i < 10 {
  io.println("Iteration running...");
  i = i + 1;  // CRITICAL: must change the condition variable!
}
```

If you forget to update `i`, the loop runs forever (infinite loop).

### Counting Down

```xiom
var fuel = 10;
while fuel > 0 {
  io.println("Fuel remaining...");
  fuel = fuel - 1;
}
io.println("Out of fuel!");
```

### Using Loops for Accumulation

```xiom
var sum = 0;
var n = 1;
while n <= 5 {
  sum = sum + n;  // 1+2+3+4+5 = 15
  n = n + 1;
}
// sum is 15
```

### While vs If

| If | While |
|----|-------|
| Runs 0 or 1 time | Runs 0 or MANY times |
| One-and-done decision | Repeating decision |
| `if hungry { eat(); }` | `while hungry { eat(); }` |

